{"candidates":[{"content":"The RESIDUE label (SRC_012) contradicts the skeptical semantics: naming a 'standing reason' for suspension makes the suspension actionable, and actionable suspension is a de facto promotion mechanism — operators will re-examine nodes whose RESIDUE names a fixable cause, which is exactly the re-argumentation the skeptical fixpoint was designed to prevent. Refuted if: there exists a graph where RESIDUE labels are not a pure function of the grounded labels and edges — e.g., two isomorphic graphs with the same grounded extension yield different RESIDUE sets.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"5431feda66e41c92d61607a482c76cb05bd782c10644d596f9f903e82e4e8c2f"},{"role":"dependence","target":"3e200f5e7e7e5a22aa8ad1de1613b1dba7dcc320ef14624b05385b9bf0699fa3"}],"typicality":0.7},{"content":"Standing residue (SRC_005) depends on the empirical parameter k, and the set of standing nodes is not k-invariant: a node with a defense path of length k+1 is 'standing' at depth k but not at depth k+1. Since k is chosen externally, the standing report is not a property of the graph — it is a property of the analyst's horizon. Refuted if: for every graph, the standing residue set is identical for all k ≥ 0.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"64c14910a04cd26ea58a8393b56d539819e3ec97491498983221e7911121a250"}],"typicality":0.6},{"content":"The closure rule (SRC_004) — every attacker of a warrant's validity_node also attacks the carriage edge — contradicts the skeptical semantics if it changes the grounded extension: materializing attack edges can flip a previously accepted node to undecided, so the 'derived' labels are not the same as the original fixpoint's labels. Refuted if: for every warrant set, the grounded extension of the closed graph is identical to the grounded extension of the original graph.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"9f8658b0e01e3bbb26471ff384410bd4edc6256b643b1a4385e0051ebff8f31b"}],"typicality":0.5},{"content":"The diagnostic overlay program (SRC_003, SRC_012, SRC_005) abstracts to the principle that every grounded-extension outcome has a local, deterministic, polynomial explanation. This abstraction is refuted if there exists an undecided node whose suspension reason is genuinely global — e.g., a node suspended only because of a cycle of mutual attacks of length O(V) where no bounded local neighborhood determines the suspension. The bounded BFS in SRC_005 would misclassify such a node as 'standing' when it is actually part of a large-scale structure that a different decomposition would explain.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"3e200f5e7e7e5a22aa8ad1de1613b1dba7dcc320ef14624b05385b9bf0699fa3"},{"role":"dependence","target":"5431feda66e41c92d61607a482c76cb05bd782c10644d596f9f903e82e4e8c2f"},{"role":"dependence","target":"64c14910a04cd26ea58a8393b56d539819e3ec97491498983221e7911121a250"}],"typicality":0.4},{"content":"SRC_003's suspend_reason and SRC_002's DefenseWitness share the mechanism of greedy post-hoc classification over final labels. This shared mechanism is compatible with determinism but is refuted if the greedy choice is non-unique: two different greedy scans (e.g., different edge iteration orders) produce different witnesses or different suspend_reason labels for the same graph, meaning the 'explanation' is an artifact of the scan order, not of the argumentation structure.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"3e200f5e7e7e5a22aa8ad1de1613b1dba7dcc320ef14624b05385b9bf0699fa3"},{"role":"dependence","target":"fcda5636ce7a286b24f0d77fa016172c8deeb573eef34924a2f18b1ae73a37e5"}],"typicality":0.3},{"content":"The fragility profile (SRC_007) reduces to the DefenseWitness computation (SRC_002): an accepted node is fragile under single-edge deletion iff deleting an edge removes a member of its greedy defense witness that is uniquely responsible for some attacker. This reduction gives fragility auditing in O(V+E) without re-running the fixpoint per edge. Refuted if: there exists a graph where an accepted node's label changes under single-edge deletion but no witness member is uniquely responsible for any attacker, so fragility is not witness-detectable.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"fcda5636ce7a286b24f0d77fa016172c8deeb573eef34924a2f18b1ae73a37e5"},{"role":"dependence","target":"b25c49bfd4585cd0824221f7fd6fa154ffed828ad96ef4adc25fe3bed6b8517c"}],"typicality":0.2}],"scratch_proposal":{"cluster_suggestions":[],"links":[],"new_blocks":[{"body":{"bears_on_refs":["SRC_003","SRC_012","SRC_005"],"content":"Speculative counterfactual: all five foundations assume the grounded extension is the correct substrate for diagnostics. But what if the grounded extension is too coarse? A node suspended due to a 3-cycle of mutual attacks has a different 'standing reason' than a node suspended due to an accepted attacker, yet both are UNDECIDED. The diagnostic overlays try to fix this, but they inherit the fixpoint's information loss: the fixpoint only outputs labels, not the reasons. So any post-hoc explanation is a reconstruction, not a recovery. This suggests the diagnostics are fundamentally underdetermined.","possible_next_move":"Try to construct a graph where two different post-hoc explanations are both consistent with the same grounded extension.","why_keep_this":"Challenges the recoverability assumption shared by SRC_003, SRC_012, SRC_005."},"local_key":"NEW_001"}],"revisions":[],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"Can a single grounded extension be generated by two different attack graphs that yield different RESIDUE annotations? If so, RESIDUE is not a function of the extension but of the graph.","related_refs":["NEW_001"]}]},"simulation_proposals":[]}